@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the searches of the Pulling Book run of 2026-09-16
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703: two branches
# appending to the end of examinations/searches.ttl do not merge without a
# hand. The register is the class and not the file.
#
# Two of the three came back empty, and one of those two is the reason the
# registration of this transmission was called the most valuable in its file.
#

pmp:YangshengContactSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Pulling Book names any source outside itself"@en, "Ob das Ziehbuch eine Quelle außerhalb seiner selbst nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Zhangjiashan Yinshu in full, all 113 slips, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any naming of a source, an authority or an origin for the regimen that lies outside the work: a teacher, a lineage, a received scripture, a foreign people, a place beyond the Chinese world, or a technical term this holding carries from the Indian material. The registration of this transmission rested on the burial date rather than on the wording, and the wording had not been checked."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole translation, case-insensitive, for: India, Indian, Buddha, Buddhist, yoga, Sanskrit, prana, foreign, barbarian, western regions, Hu, monk, master, teacher, transmitted, received from, handed down. Every occurrence of a personal name in the translation read in its place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Not one of the terms occurs. The work names exactly one person, in its first sentence, and names him as the owner of the way rather than as a teacher who handed it on: in the spring generate, in the summer grow, in the autumn harvest, in the winter preserve, this is the way of Peng Zu. Everything else it grounds in heaven, earth and the four seasons. There is no colophon, no line of transmission, no scripture cited and no authority appealed to."@en ;
    skos:note "What the empty result is worth, and what it is not worth. It does not prove that nothing reached this material from outside; an absence of names is what most technical manuals of any period show. What it does is remove the one thing that would have settled the question the other way. The manuscript went into a tomb between 186 and 156 before this era and came out in 1984, so a route of contact, if there is one, has to be argued from the practices themselves and cannot be read off a citation. For a holding whose every convergence so far has been open to the objection of reception, that is the condition it has been looking for and it is why this corpus was opened before the two that were not reachable."@en ;
    skos:note "Where a later run should go next with this. The two Mawangdui manuscripts named in the registration, the Daoyin tu and the Quegu shiqi, are from the same century and the same practice, and neither was reachable in a public-domain or freely readable English edition in this run. They would double the witness. The Huangting jing would not: it is centuries later and stands downstream of the traffic the early manuscripts stand before."@en .

pmp:YangshengPrerequisiteSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether one practice of the Pulling Book is made the condition of another"@en, "Ob eine Praxis des Ziehbuchs zur Bedingung einer anderen gemacht wird"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Zhangjiashan Yinshu in full, all 113 slips, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage making one of the work's practices the prerequisite of another: an exercise to be mastered before a second may be attempted, a purification before the breath, a stage that cannot be reached without a prior one. Prerequisite chains are one of the four kinds the harvest owes, and this holding carries them in quantity from the Chinese material of later centuries, so their absence in the earliest witness had to be established rather than assumed."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole translation for: before, first, only after, unless, must, cannot, not until, when you have, having. Every hit read in its place, and the three texts read through in order for an unmarked sequence."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "Order is everywhere in the work and it is always order inside one prescription. Extend the left foot, then raise the right arm; do the counted part, then hold the breath until the sweat comes; on rising make the movement wide, on lying down pull as far as it will go. Every occurrence of must governs a treatment and not a career: at this moment you must treat it with the pull of the Eight Warps. Nothing says that a practitioner who has not done one thing may not do another."@en ;
    skos:note "The finding is about the shape of the transmission and not about a gap in the reading. A corpus of forty-one exercises each indexed to a complaint does not need a ladder, because nothing in it is reserved: the entry condition for any one of them is having the complaint it treats. The graded sequences this holding carries from the Baopuzi, from the Shangqing material and from the internal alchemy are later, and they come with what this text has none of — a lineage, a transmission restricted to the worthy, and an attainment at the top. An exhibited gap here is therefore worth more than a found passage would have been."@en .

pmp:YangshengSignOfSuccessSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Pulling Book names a perceptible sign that a treatment has worked"@en, "Ob das Ziehbuch ein wahrnehmbares Zeichen des Gelingens nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The Zhangjiashan Yinshu in full, all 113 slips, in Vivienne Lo (trans.), How to do the Gibbon Walk: A Translation of the Pulling Book (ca. 186 BCE), Needham Research Institute Working Papers 3 (Cambridge: Needham Research Institute, 2014)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A passage telling the practitioner what he will perceive when a treatment has taken effect, as against how many times to do it. The five kinds the harvest owes count signs of success separately, and this holding has had to record their absence in several corpora with full capacity catalogues."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "Term search of the whole translation for: sweat, sign, know, feel, appears, until, then the, will not, cured, recover, better, warm, light. Every hit read in its place; the stopping formulae of all forty-one exercises compared."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:YinshuHanChu ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "One sign and it is a sign of the procedure rather than of the cure. Under held breath, after a while, sweat comes out of the patterns of the skin, and that is the point at which the instruction turns from a count to as long as possible. Everything else stops at a number: ten times, thirty, three hundred, a thousand, or ten days. The distinction matters for what can be checked. Sweat under a held breath follows from doing the thing correctly, so a practitioner who sees it knows he performed the procedure and knows nothing yet about his neck."@en ;
    skos:note "The second-nearest thing was read and set aside as not a sign. Three outcomes are stated of the wall-and-staff treatment as things that will be the case when the qi of the head flows downward — the foot not numb, the head not swollen, the nose not blocked — and they are the complaints themselves in the negative. A transmission that says the treatment works when the symptom is gone has named a criterion and not a sign, and the difference is that a criterion of that kind is the ordinary one any medicine offers."@en .
